<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Condor Legion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Condor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> was a unit of German air and ground forces sent by Hitler to fight for Franco's Nationalists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. Made up mainly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> aircraft and crews, it gained combat experience and tested new tactics and aircraft, and its bombing of the Basque town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Guernica</a> in April 1937 became a symbol of the horrors of modern war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/condor-legion.jpg" alt="Condor Legion" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Condor_Legion_marching_during_the_Spanish_Civil_War.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the military coup of 17 July 1936 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, the Nationalists asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for support. The first request for German aircraft, on 22 July, was for ten transport planes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> decided to help on 25 or 26 July, though he was wary of provoking a wider European war. The first mission, Operation Magic Fire, used Junkers Ju 52 aircraft to carry the Spanish Army of Africa from Spanish Morocco to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and German involvement was hidden through a dummy company and funded with three million Reichsmarks. The first contingent of 86 men left Germany on 1 August 1936, many of them believing they would only train the Nationalists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> was commanded by Hugo Sperrle, with Wilhelm Ritter von Thoma leading its ground element, which included small armoured units of Panzer I tanks. It had bomber, fighter, reconnaissance, anti-aircraft and signals groups. In 1937 it supported the Nationalist offensive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>, a Basque region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its aircraft attacked Otxandio and Durango, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> said 250 civilians died on 31 March, and on 26 April it bombed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Guernica</a> in Operation Rügen. Casualties there are disputed: perhaps 200 or 300 people were killed, while the Basques reported 1,654 dead. The Times reporter George Steer wrote the first full account, noting German involvement.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> also fought at the Battle of Brunete and in the campaign that ended with the fall of Santander and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gijon">Gijón</a> on 21 October 1937. After the Nationalist conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, much of its industrial and mineral output was shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> as repayment for the Legion's service. Its maritime unit attacked Republican shipping and ports. The Legion left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in May 1939, sailing from Vigo to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, where crowds and ceremonies awaited it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Many have regarded the intervention as a proving ground for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Hermann Göring said at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> that he urged Hitler to help Franco partly to test the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a>. The Messerschmitt Bf 109 entered service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from about January 1937, and Wolfram von Richthofen, who commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>, later led ground-attack units in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a>. Adolf Galland, however, held that the value of the lessons was exaggerated and the wrong conclusions drawn. In 1997 German President Roman Herzog apologised to survivors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Guernica</a> raid, and in 1998 the German parliament had the names of former Legion members removed from military bases.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Condor Legion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Condor Legion</b> fought for Franco in the Spanish Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Condor Legion</b>, with Italian support, bombed the Basque town on market day, killing hundreds of civilians.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-26">26 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
